  "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the area of the heptagon \\(AFNBCEM\\), given that the area of quadrilateral \\(DEGF\\) is 288. Here's a step-by-step approach:\n\n### Step 1: Understand the given information\n- Points \\(A, D, E, B\\) lie on side \\(\\overline{AB}\\) with \\(AD = 4\\), \\(DE = 16\\), and \\(EB = 8\\).\n- Points \\(A, F, G, C\\) lie on side \\(\\overline{AC}\\) with \\(AF = 13\\), \\(FG = 52\\), and \\(GC = 26\\).\n- \\(M\\) is the reflection of \\(D\\) through \\(F\\).\n- \\(N\\) is the reflection of \\(G\\) through \\(E\\).\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the lengths of \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\)\nSince \\(A, D, E, B\\) lie on \\(\\overline{AB}\\):\n\\[\nAB = AD + DE + EB = 4 + 16 + 8 = 28\n\\]\nSince \\(A, F, G, C\\) lie on \\(\\overline{AC}\\):\n\\[\nAC = AF + FG + GC = 13 + 52 + 26 = 91\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Analyze the quadrilateral \\(DEGF\\)\nThe area of quadrilateral \\(DEGF\\) is given as 288. Since \\(M\\) is the reflection of \\(D\\) through \\(F\\), \\(M\\) lies on the line \\(DE\\) extended beyond \\(E\\). Similarly, \\(N\\) is the reflection of \\(G\\) through \\(E\\), so \\(N\\) lies on the line \\(FG\\) extended beyond \\(G\\).\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the area of the heptagon \\(AFNBCEM\\)\nThe heptagon \\(AFNBCEM\\) can be divided into the quadrilateral \\(DEGF\\) and the triangle \\(AFN\\). Since \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are reflections, the area of \\(AFNBCEM\\) is twice the area of \\(DEGF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } AFNBCEM = 2 \\times \\text{Area of } DEGF = 2 \\times 288 = 576\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{576}\n\\]",
